A||||6||DJRice||Changing the Water Pump on V6
Z||000000||DJRice||08-23-99||07:50 AM||||I did a tune up on my 87SE V6 this weekend, and was suprised to find that the lazy previous owner had never changed the forward 3 spark plugs.  They were pretty well rusted in place.  A new set of Bosch Platinums with a new dist. cap and rotor solved my sluggish start problem mentioned in another thread.<p>Anyway, in the process of doing a brake job and tune up, I noticed I had a bit of a drip forming.  It turns out I am going to be in the market for a new Water Pump (maybe next weekend-but its a real slow leak).  So, are there any tips for making this job as easy as it can be.<p>My current living situation, (apartment complex) forces me to either pay a mechanic to work on it, or pack up for the weekend and go visit my parents about 3 hrs away where I have access to all the tools I would need.  Any tips on making this as painless as possible are greatly appreciated.  
Z||000001||DJRice||08-23-99||07:52 AM||||Although I just finished a major tune up, If this is the best time for me to do that Northstar conversion (since Im going to have the water pump out anyway) let me know.....<p>HeHe, I wish <IMG SRC="/ubb/wink.gif" WIDTH=15 HEIGHT=15>
Z||000002||BENGAL4||08-23-99||01:06 PM||||D.J.,<p>I just finished putting a timing set on my V6 in which I had to remove the waterpump to get to everything else. It,s pretty easy as long as you remove the inner skirting on the fender well. This will make accessing the bolts easier. I would even take the crank pully off if I could.  It should take about 2 hours to complete the job. When you are refilling with coolant, dont forget the special procedure. Take the front radiator cap off and fill from the back thermostat housing until it is running out of the radiator neck. Go buy a Haynes manual and between that and our great freinds on the forum, you will have no problems.<p><br>GO BENGALS 
Z||000003||theogre||08-23-99||02:11 PM||||driving 3 hours with a leaking pump probably is not a good idea.<p>Unlike many cars, you can't run Fiero with the cap loose to keep it from presurizing the system. If the thing fails to hold presure on the way up,,, your stuck a long way from home.
Z||000004||sluppy123||08-23-99||08:07 PM||||I'm in the process of "changing" the water pump on my 87' GT "I'm in the 5th week". It all started about 5 weeks ago while I was on my way home from work and I heard a grinding noise coming from the engine. I hurried home and saw that my baby was bleeding anti-freeze. Not having time to fix it myself I took it into the local shop the next morning. Around 4:00 I get a call from the shop saying they had some problems getting the old waterpump off, they broke 4 bolts off in the block and twisted the head off another & for $600 they would be glad to pull the engine a remove the bolts. After saying a few colorful words I told him he could just roll it outside and I would have it towed to my house.<p>With 160k on it I guess it was a good way to start a complete rebuild. And now five week's later My baby is sitting in the garage with a New Long block bolted to the cradle and waiting to be slide back under the car.<br>     I hope your luck is a little better<br>                                 -Brian
Z||000005||TOM||08-23-99||10:27 PM||||Wait a minute. You went through the cost and headache of an engine rebuild for 5 broken bolts. Was the engine dieing anyway? Why didnt you just fix the bolts and install the new water pump?
Z||000006||DJRice||08-24-99||07:11 AM||||Oddly enough, It is only an intermittent leak.  I spotted it once when I went to visit my aunt and then got worried.  After that I looked for it and it would not leak.  Then as soon as I forget about it, it starts to drip again.  I wouldnt have driven it back home if it was more than several drips.  Believe me, if it was grinding and not just dripping a bit, I would not have driven it.<p>I had to have a hose replaced by a mechanic down here 2 months ago and it cost me $85.  I hated having to pay someone else to install a $15 hose, but I am just not equipped to deal with car repair where I am now.<p>Sluppy, I definintely hope I dont have your problem.....Of course, if bolts get broken, I can only blame myself....
